Presidential elections were held in [[Georgia (country)|Georgia]] on 5 January 2008,<ref>[http://www.itar-tass.com/eng/level2.html?NewsID=12050251&PageNum=0 Georgia to hold presidential election on Jan 5, 2008] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071224050808/http://www.itar-tass.com/eng/level2.html?NewsID=12050251&PageNum=0 |date=2007-12-24 }} [[Itar-Tass]]. November 8, 2007.</ref> moved forward from autumn 2008 by [[President of Georgia|President]] [[Mikheil Saakashvili]] after the [[2007 Georgian demonstrations|2007 demonstrations]].<ref>[http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/europe/7085097.stm Georgia to hold early elections]. [[BBC News]]. November 8, 2007.</ref>

A double [[2008 Georgian referendum|referendum]] on when to hold the [[2008 Georgian parliamentary election|legislative elections]] and on [[NATO]] membership was held on the same date.<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16434 Georgia to Hold Plebiscite on NATO Membership]. Civil Georgia. 2007-11-26.</ref>

Saakashvili was declared the winner with 53.7% of the votes, despite accusations of [[electoral fraud]] from the Georgian opposition.<ref>{{cite web|title=Saakashvili officially declared winner in Georgian presidential election |publisher=[[Interfax]] |date=2008-01-13 |url=http://www.interfax.ru/e/B/politics/28.html?id_issue=11944114 |accessdate=2008-01-13 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20110525090922/http://www.interfax.ru/e/B/politics/28.html?id_issue=11944114 |archivedate=May 25, 2011 }}</ref> International observers welcomed the elections as "the first genuinely competitive presidential election" in the history of Georgia,<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2009/04/10/opinion/10iht-edlet.html | title=Georgia: On the Right Track | date=April 10, 2009 | work=[[The New York Times]] | accessdate=2018-01-05}}</ref> and said, despite observed irregularities, the elections generally met the democratic standards.<ref>{{cite news|author=Tom Parfitt|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2008/jan/07/georgia.international|title=Opposition claims Georgia president rigged election victory|newspaper=Guardian|date=7 January 2008|accessdate=7 January 2013}}</ref>

==Background==
In November 2007, tens of thousands of Georgians protested outside the parliament in the capital, Tbilisi, urging President Mikhail Saakashvili to step down. The crowd also called for early parliamentary elections. They accused Saakashvili of heading a corrupt, authoritarian government and wanted him to be ousted democratically.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/7074585.stm |work=BBC News | title=Mass protest in Georgian capital | date=3 November 2007 | access-date=22 April 2010| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100420233228/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/7074585.stm| archive-date= 20 April 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref> The protests turned violent when the [[Law enforcement in Georgia (country)|police]] used tear gas and water cannons to dislodge the protesters from the territory adjoining to the [[Parliament of Georgia|Parliament building]], and prevented the demonstrators from resuming the protests. The government accused the Russian secret services of being involved in an attempted coup d'état and declared a nationwide [[state of emergency]], which lasted until 16 November 2007. In addition, on 8 November, President Saakashvili announced a compromise solution to hold an early presidential election on 5 January 2008. Pursuant to the [[Constitution of Georgia (country)|Constitution of Georgia]], Saakashvili resigned on 25 November to launch his pre-election campaign.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16425 |title=Saakashvili Steps Down, as Parliament Calls for Early Polls |publisher=Civil.ge |access-date=2 June 2015}}</ref>

In late December, Badri Patarkatsishvili, a business tycoon and presidential candidate who had pledged his financial support to the November rallies, became embroiled in a major controversy. The authorities released a series of audio and video recordings of two separate meetings between a high-ranking Interior Ministry official Erekle Kodua, Patarkatsishvili himself and the head of his pre-election campaign, Valeri Gelbakhiani. According to these materials, Patarkatsishvili attempted to bribe Kodua into claiming voter fraud and taking part in what the Georgian officials described as an attempted coup d'état on January 6, 2008, the day after the scheduled presidential elections. The plan included staging a mass manifestation against the government and "neutralizing" the Interior Minister [[Vano Merabishvili]]. Patarkatsishvili confirmed that he met with Kodua in London, but denied the bribe was in connection to a coup plot. Instead, he claimed his intention was to uncover official plans to rig the election. He also confirmed that he had offered Kodua "a huge amount of money" in exchange for defecting from the authorities to avert the possible use of government force against the planned January rallies.<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16653 Patarkatsishvili Ally a Suspect in Coup Plot]. Civil Georgia / 2007-12-24 13:22:55.</ref><ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16668 More ‘Coup Plot’ Tapes Released]. Civil Georgia, Tbilisi / 2007-12-25 20:33:27.</ref><ref>[http://www.geotimes.ge/index.php?m=home&newsid=8395 Patarkatsishvili Forced onto Defensive] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080830041243/http://www.geotimes.ge/index.php?m=home&newsid=8395 |date=August 30, 2008 }}. Georgian Times [Civil Georgia]. 2007.12.26 12:52.</ref> On December 26, 2007, several leading journalists defected from [[Imedi TV]], co-owned by Patarkatsishvili. Later that day, the television station’s management announced that Imedi TV temporarily suspended broadcasts until the station's "legal status in respect of ownership is clarified." "By doing so we are distancing from dirty political games", said Giorgi Targamadze, head of the Imedi TV's political programs.<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16679 Imedi TV Suspends Broadcasts].  Civil Georgia, Tbilisi. 2007-12-26.</ref> The opposition politicians formerly allied with him also made attempts to distance themselves from Patarkatsishvili and condemned what they described as illegal methods used by both the authorities and "other forces," apparently referring to Patarkatsishvili.<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16699 Authorities Attack Nine-Party Coalition over Patarkatsishvili Ties]. Civil Georgia, 2007-12-27.</ref>

On December 28, 2007, Patarkatsishvili announced that he would withdraw his bid for presidency, but would remain a candidate until January 4, 2008.<ref>{{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20070611065820/http://www.civil.ge/eng/detail.php?id=16704|title=Patarkatsishvili Nominally Remains in Race}}. Civil Georgia. 2007-12-28.</ref> On January 3, 2008, he reversed himself and decided to run in presidential elections. In response, his top campaign official Giorgi Zhvania (brother of the late Prime Minister [[Zurab Zhvania]]) resigned, declaring that Patarkasishvili does not have a reputation one would expect of a country's president.<ref>[http://www.voanews.com/english/2008-01-03-voa70.cfm Georgian Media Tycoon Returns to Presidential Race] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080107081724/http://www.voanews.com/english/2008-01-03-voa70.cfm |date=January 7, 2008 }}. [[Voice of America]]. January 03, 2008.</ref>

Meanwhile, the [[Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe]] (OSCE) released two interim reports on the election campaign, saying that the "legal framework [was] generally favorable to the conduct of democratic elections in Georgia, if implemented in good faith." However, they expressed concerns about "a highly polarized political environment" within the country's political spectrum, including the allegations of Saakashvili's use of administrative resources and the lack of balance in [[Georgian media]].<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng_/article.php?id=16642 OSCE Interim Report on Election Campaign]. Civil Georgia, December 21, 2007.</ref><ref>[http://www.osce.org/documents/odihr/2007/12/29149_en.pdf Interim Report No 2]. The OSCE Observer Mission. December 28, 2007.</ref>

On December 28, 2007, Saakashvili vowed to lead Georgia into NATO and to restore its territorial integrity in his second term if reelected. He stated he would hand over a united Georgia to his successor after the end of his second term.<ref>[http://english.people.com.cn/90001/90777/6329507.html Georgian president vows to join NATO if reelected – People's Daily Online<!-- Bot generated title -->]</ref>

The pre-election period in Georgia was also marked with rising tensions in breakaway [[Abkhazia]]. Early in January 2008, the Georgian media reported attacks on ethnic Georgians in the [[Gali District, Abkhazia|Gali district]] controlled by the de facto Abkhaz administration. The reports said that the Georgians living in Abkhazia were being intimidated by local Abkhaz officials to prevent them from participating in Georgia's presidential election. At least seven houses owned by ethnic Georgians were destroyed by fire. Although Abkhaz officials rejected the accusations,<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16753 Sokhumi Denies Reported Attacks on Georgians in Gali]. Civil Georgia. January 4, 2008.</ref> the acting Georgian president [[Nino Burjanadze]] warned that attempts were being made to add conflict on the eve of the election.<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16755 Attempts Made to Raise Tension in Conflict Zones – Burjanadze]. Civil Georgia. January 4, 2008.</ref>

==Candidates==
[[Badri Patarkatsishvili]], a [[business oligarch]] who made a fortune in [[Russia]], announced he would be a candidate on 10 November 2007.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.anatoliantimes.com/hbr2.asp?id=&s=int&a=071110131405.sh2u8tvh|title=The Anatolian Times<!-- Bot generated title -->|publisher=|accessdate=3 January 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071224192218/http://www.anatoliantimes.com/hbr2.asp?id=&s=int&a=071110131405.sh2u8tvh|archive-date=24 December 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref> The opposition parties stated they would nominate a single candidate, who would have a "great chance of winning the election." The nominee would not be Patarkatsishvili, former Defence Minister [[Irakli Okruashvili]] or the activist [[Tina Khidasheli]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://eng.primenewsonline.com/news/121/ARTICLE/16760/2007-11-09.html |title=Zurabishvili: Opposition To Nominate One Candidate For Presidential Elections<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2007-11-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071110182102/http://eng.primenewsonline.com/news/121/ARTICLE/16760/2007-11-09.html |archive-date=2007-11-10 |url-status=dead }}</ref> On November 12, the opposition parties nominated MP [[Levan Gachechiladze]], a leader of the [[2007 Georgian demonstrations]], as their common candidate.<ref>[http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/7091159.stm "Georgia poll challenger nominated"], BBC News, November 12, 2007.</ref> The [[Georgian Labour Party]] supported its leader [[Shalva Natelashvili]] as a candidate instead of Gachechiladze, and the [[New Right (Georgia)|New Right]] nominated MP [[Davit Gamkrelidze]] as their candidate.

Saakashvili was nominated as his party's candidate on 23 November.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.africasia.com/services/news/newsitem.php?area=mideast&item=071123150308.s61i9m60.php |title=IC Publications<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2007-11-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120207050919/http://www.africasia.com/services/news/newsitem.php?area=mideast&item=071123150308.s61i9m60.php |archive-date=2012-02-07 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Twenty-two citizens of Georgia expressed willingness to run in the elections. According to the Georgian election code each of them had to submit at least 50,000 signatures of supporters in order to be registered by the Central Election Commission as official candidates.<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16433 22 Bid for Presidency – CEC]. Civil Georgia. 2007-11-26.</ref>

In total, thirteen candidates submitted signatures, but only seven were recognized by the Central Election Commission (CEC) as eligible to run for the presidency: 
* [[Levan Gachechiladze]], nominated by the nine-party opposition coalition
* [[Davit Gamkrelidze]], leader of the [[New Right (Georgia)|New Right]]
* [[Gia Maisashvili]], leader of the [[Party of the Future (Georgia)|Party of the Future]]
* [[Shalva Natelashvili]], leader of the [[Georgian Labour Party]]
* [[Badri Patarkatsishvili]], a business and media tycoon
* [[Mikheil Saakashvili]], the ex-president and the leader of ruling [[United National Movement (Georgia)|United National Movement]]
* [[Irina Sarishvili-Chanturia]], the only female presidential candidate and the leader of the Russian-leaning [[Hope Party]].<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16557 Seven Remain in Presidential Race]. Civil Georgia. 2007-12-11.</ref>

==Opinion polls==
In a November 2007 pre-election poll held by the weekly ''Mteli Kvira'', the opposition candidate Gachechiladze defeated Saakashvili by 2% (18% to 16%).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.interfax.ru/e/B/politics/28.html?id_issue=11912685|title=Интерфакс: новости|accessdate=3 January 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071224213205/http://www.interfax.ru/e/B/politics/28.html?id_issue=11912685|archive-date=24 December 2007|url-status=dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/29127/low_numbers_for_candidates_in_georgia |title=Low Numbers for Candidates in Georgia: Angus Reid Global Monitor<!-- Bot generated title --> |access-date=2007-11-24 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071224074525/http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/29127/low_numbers_for_candidates_in_georgia |archive-date=2007-12-24 |url-status=usurped }}</ref>

In a December 2007 poll commissioned by Saakashvili's party, the BCG company surveyed 13,000 respondents throughout Georgia and showed that 29.5% of voters were still undecided. 36.7% said they would vote for Saakashvili, followed by Gachechiladze with 9.7%; Patarkatsishvili – 4.7%; Gamkrelidze – 3%; Natelashvili – 2.5%; Maisashvili and Sarishvili had less than 1% each. One percent said they would vote for none of the candidates. The survey showed that 63.5% of those who had decided to vote for one of the candidates would vote for Saakashvili, followed by Gachechiladze and Patarkatsishvili with 16.7% and 8.1%, respectively.<ref name="survey">[http://www.civil.ge/eng/article.php?id=16652 Imedi TV Plans Exit Polls]. Civil Georgia. 2007-12-23.</ref>

Another survey, also commissioned by Saakashvili's party, was overseen by the U.S. based [[Greenberg Quinlan Rosner Research]], but conducted by the Georgian ACT group. This survey involved 1,500 respondents and found that 41% would vote for Saakashvili, followed by Gachechiladze – 11.1%; Patarkatsishvili – 6.5%; Natelashvili – 3.5%; Gamkrelidze – 2.1%; Maisashvili and Sarishvili – less than 1% each. 20.6% were undecided and 2.3% said they wouldn’t vote for any candidate. Of those who had decided to vote for one of the candidates, 64% said they would vote for Saakashvili, followed by Gachechiladze and Patarkatsishvili with 17% and 10%, respectively.<ref name="survey"/>

On December 23, 2007, the pro-opposition [[Imedi TV]] announced that an organization called Dialogue for Development of Democracy had conducted an opinion poll between December 17 and December 21. The survey showed that 22.1% of the 2,100 surveyed would support Levan Gachechiladze, followed by Mikheil Saakashvili with 20.3%; Badri Patarkatsishvili – 19.1%; Shalva Natelashvili – 6.5%; Davit Gamkrelidze – 4.9%; Giorgi Maisashvili – 1.1% and Irina Sarishvili – 0.2%. The survey reported that 21.7% remained undecided.<ref name="survey"/>

A survey, commissioned once again by Saakashvili’s campaign from Greenberg Quinlan Rosner, was published on January 3, 2008. It showed that Saakashvili had the support of 42 percent, compared to 19 percent for Levan Gachechiladze, 11 percent for Badri Patarkatsishvili, 5 percent for Shalva Natelashvili, 4 percent for David Gamkrelidze, and 1 percent for Gia Maisashvili; 2 percent would not vote or vote blank, and 16 percent were undecided. The survey reported only a minority of Georgian voters felt the presidential elections would not be fair.<ref>[http://www.greenbergresearch.com/index.php?ID=2121 Survey Shows Saakashvili with Strong Lead in Presidential Ballot] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110711124523/http://www.greenbergresearch.com/index.php?ID=2121 |date=2011-07-11 }}. Greenberg Quinlan Rosner, January 3, 2008.</ref>

==Conduct==
In addition to local watchdogs, 29 international or foreign organizations (including OSCE, [[Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe]], and [[International Crisis Group]]) observed the elections.<ref>[http://www.civil.ge/eng_/article.php?id=16689 International Observers]. Civil Georgia.</ref>

Early on election day all polling stations were opened with the exception of the highland village of [[Shatili]] where heavy snow thwarted the process.<ref>{{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20070611065820/http://www.civil.ge/eng_/article.php?id=16763|title=All Polling Stations Opened, Except of One in Shatili}}. Civil Georgia, January 5, 2008.</ref>

==Exit polls==
All major national television broadcasters planned to conduct their own exit polls and commissioned seven local research groups.<ref name="survey"/>

The first [[exit poll]] results were conflicting:
* According to a survey commissioned by 4 TV stations ([[Georgian Public Broadcaster]], [[Rustavi 2]], Mze, and Achara TV) from the Georgian Institute of Public Affairs (GIPA), [[Ilia Chavchavadze State University]] and two think-tanks – the [[Caucasus Institute for Peace, Democracy and Development]] (CIPDD) and the [[Georgian Foundation for Strategic and International Studies]] (GFSIS), Mikheil Saakashvili was winning with a narrow absolute majority of 53.5% of the votes, with Levan Gachechiladze coming second with 29.1%.<ref>[http://www.archevnebi.ge/index.php?lang=eng Exitpoll 2008<!-- Bot generated title -->]{{Dead link|date=July 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> Voter turnout was 46.4%.<ref>[http://eng.primenewsonline.com/news/121/ARTICLE/17969/2008-01-05.html] {{Webarchive|url=https://archive.today/20120707232222/http://eng.primenewsonline.com/news/121/ARTICLE/17969/2008-01-05.html |date=2012-07-07 }} Prime News Agency statement</ref> Twenty-three percent of respondents refused to say for whom they had voted. The poll had a 2% [[margin of error]]. The figures were provisional, with final results not expected for another few hours.<ref>{{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20070611065820/http://www.civil.ge/eng_/article.php?id=16788|title=Early Exit Poll Results Show Saakashvili in Lead}}. Civil Georgia. January 5, 2008.</ref>
* According to a relatively unknown<ref>[https://civil.ge/archives/113889 'Do not Trust Exit Polls’ – Nine-Party Opposition Coalition]. Civil Georgia. January 3, 2008.</ref> Ukrainian think tank "Common European Cause", which claimed to have interviewed 10,000 people at 200 polling stations, Gachechiladze won the most votes (31%), followed by Saakashvili (24.4%) and Patarkatsishvili (20.3%).<ref>{{cite web
 |title       = Ukrainian think-tank: None of presidential contenders in Georgia obtained more than 50% of votes
 |publisher   = [[REGNUM News Agency]]
 |date        = 2008-01-05
 |url         = http://www.regnum.ru/english/939470.html
 |accessdate  = 2008-01-05
 |url-status     = dead
 |archiveurl  = https://web.archive.org/web/20080108063117/http://www.regnum.ru/english/939470.html
 |archivedate = 2008-01-08
}}</ref>

==Results==
The Central Election Committee stated the turnout was 56.17%, or 1,912,943 voters. As announced by the Central Election Committee on 20:00 (16:00 GMT) of January 6, data from 2,605 precincts had been counted and showed Saakashvili in the lead with 51.95% of the votes, and Gachechiladze in second place with 25.14%.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.civil.ge/eng_/category.php?id=32 |title=Archived copy |access-date=2007-12-28 |archive-date=2023-04-07 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230407231211/https://www.civil.ge/eng_/category.php?id=32 |url-status=dead }}</ref> 2 days later, with votes from more polling stations having been counted, Saakashvili was leading with 52.21%,<ref name=autogenerated1>{{cite web|url=http://www.results.cec.gov.ge/|title=შედეგები 2016 – მეორე ტური|publisher=|accessdate=3 January 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131102080202/http://results.cec.gov.ge/|archive-date=2 November 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> Gachechiladze following him with 25.26% of the votes.<ref name=autogenerated1 /> On 9 January 2008, with 98.8% of the ballots counted, Saakashvili had 52.21%, meaning he could not fall below the 50% which would result in a run-off.<ref>{{Cite web |url= http://www.afp.com/english/news/stories/080109074406.g2egp1zx.html |title=AFP.com {{!}} Agence France-Presse, a global news agency |access-date= 2008-01-11 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120728212913/http://www.afp.com/english/news/stories/080109074406.g2egp1zx.html |archive-date= 2012-07-28 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

